1. Project Overview
1.1 What are we building?
We are building a machine-learning pipeline that estimates the
Remaining Useful Life (RUL) of a simulated aircraft turbofan engine
from its historical sensor readings.

RUL means the estimated number of operating cycles remaining before an
engine reaches its end-of-life point. In this project, the model will
learn relationships between engine sensor measurements, operating
conditions, and RUL labels.
1.2 Why is this useful?
This is an example of predictive maintenance. Instead of relying
only on fixed maintenance schedules or waiting for a failure, a
predictive-maintenance system uses observed condition data to estimate
when maintenance may be needed.
1.3 Dataset choice
The planned dataset is NASA C-MAPSS FD004. C-MAPSS is a simulated
turbofan-engine degradation dataset. FD004 includes multiple operating
conditions and fault modes, making the prediction task more challenging
than a simple static regression problem.

The project plan records the commonly reported FD004 sizes as: - 248
training engines - 249 test engines - 6 operating conditions - 2 fault
modes - 21 sensor measurements - Approximately 61,249 training
observations - Approximately 41,214 test observations

These counts should be verified against the actual files when the
dataset is downloaded and loaded.

Important limitation: This is an academic project using simulated
data. Its predictions are not certified real-world aircraft maintenance
advice.
1.4 Planned final outcome
The intended project will: 1. Load and validate the raw data. 2. Perform
exploratory data analysis (EDA). 3. Create RUL labels for training
observations. 4. Engineer current-cycle and rolling-window features. 5.
Split data by engine ID to reduce leakage. 6. Train and compare
regression models. 7. Evaluate with regression metrics and the NASA
C-MAPSS score. 8. Analyze errors and visualize predictions. 9. Provide a
prediction script and optionally a Streamlit demo.
