4. Python Environment
4.1 Why use a virtual environment?
A virtual environment keeps this project's installed Python packages
separate from system Python and other projects. This reduces dependency
conflicts and makes the project easier to reproduce.

The environment directory is named .venv.
4.2 Create and activate it
From the root of the project:
python3.12 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
The terminal prompt should show the environment name, usually (.venv).

Check the Python version and executable:
python --version
which python
Expected: - Python reports version 3.12.x. - The executable path points
inside the project's .venv directory.
4.3 Dependency installation
The project uses requirements.txt to list packages needed by the
environment.

Recommended installation commands:
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
Using python -m pip helps ensure pip belongs to the currently active
Python interpreter.
